  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 313 AM EDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nDaily Storm Chances and a Slight Warming Trend:\n\nNorth and Central Georgia will remain in moist southwest flow mid-\nweek as a cutoff mid-level low remains parked over the TN Valley. \nLarge scale ascent from this system will support another day of \nnumerous to widespread showers and storms, with highest coverage \nacross northern areas. The low will finally slide north Thursday as \nit gets squeezed between a ridge pushing in from the Western \nAtlantic, and broad troughing across the Central US. Coverage of \nshowers and storms will decrease, becoming scattered to numerous \nthrough Friday as plenty of moisture and instability remains over \nthe region. While no widespread severe weather is expected, a few \nstrong storms will be possible during the period, with strong winds \nas the primary threat, in addition to localized flash flooding from \nthe high moisture content. Model guidance remains in good agreement \non this typical summertime pattern persisting into next weekend. \nHigh temperatures will initially be in the 80s to low 90s mid-week \ndue to increased cloud cover and proximity of the mid-level low, but \nwill gradually warm up to the low to mid 90s by the weekend as the \nridge builds in.",
